1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,472
Documents attached to a content item hold words that people look for.

2
00:00:05,472 --> 00:00:09,944
This video shows how the Media Indexing features add the text of PDF,

3
00:00:09,944 --> 00:00:14,535
Word, PowerPoint and text files to a search index, so site search finds

4
00:00:14,535 --> 00:00:16,980
the content item that carries them.

5
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:25,929
A search index holds the text of content items, such as a title and a body.

6
00:00:25,929 --> 00:00:29,633
The words inside a file attached to a content item are not part

7
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:32,640
of that text, so searching for them finds nothing.

8
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:37,852
Three features read files and hand their text to the index.

9
00:00:37,852 --> 00:00:43,687
PDF Media Indexing reads PDF files, Text Media Indexing reads text and Markdown

10
00:00:43,687 --> 00:00:48,630
files, and OpenXML Media Indexing reads Word and PowerPoint files.

11
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:50,991
Three conditions apply.

12
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:54,824
The file has to be attached through a Media Field, since a file

13
00:00:54,824 --> 00:00:57,990
that only sits in the Media Library is not indexed.

14
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:01,823
The field is included in the index, and an index such as Lucene

15
00:01:01,823 --> 00:01:03,490
covers the content type.

16
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:12,027
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for media indexing.

17
00:01:12,027 --> 00:01:17,655
In the Search category, the list shows PDF Media Indexing, Text Media Indexing,

18
00:01:17,655 --> 00:01:19,840
and OpenXML Media Indexing.

19
00:01:20,298 --> 00:01:24,631
Let's select the three features, open Bulk Actions, and choose Enable.

20
00:01:24,631 --> 00:01:29,078
All three depend on Media Indexing, which is enabled together with them.

21
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:35,341
After the page reloads, searching again shows all three features with the Enabled status.

22
00:01:39,521 --> 00:01:42,260
Media indexing works field by field.

23
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:47,871
Let's open Design, Content Definition, Content Types, and edit the Article type.

24
00:01:48,329 --> 00:01:53,640
Let's select Add Field, name the field Documents, choose the Media Field type,

25
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:55,789
and select Save & Configure.

26
00:01:57,483 --> 00:02:02,278
On the settings page, the Lucene Index settings section has the checkbox Include

27
00:02:02,278 --> 00:02:04,062
this element in the index.

28
00:02:04,062 --> 00:02:08,745
Let's select it, and leave Keyword cleared, because it indexes the whole value

29
00:02:08,745 --> 00:02:10,083
as a single token.

30
00:02:10,545 --> 00:02:15,902
The same page has Allow media text, which lets editors type a description for each file.

31
00:02:15,902 --> 00:02:21,431
The box is selected when the field is added, and the descriptions are indexed as MediaText.

32
00:02:21,431 --> 00:02:24,395
Let's leave it as it is and save the settings.

33
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:33,902
Let's open Content, Content Items, select the New menu, choose Article, and enter

34
00:02:33,902 --> 00:02:36,080
the title Autumn Garden Guide.

35
00:02:40,731 --> 00:02:44,993
The Documents field has an Add media button, which opens Select Media.

36
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:49,255
Let's pick the garden planting guide PDF from the library, a file that

37
00:02:49,255 --> 00:02:51,891
mentions a persimmon tree, and choose OK.

38
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:54,095
Let's select Publish.

39
00:02:54,095 --> 00:02:59,240
The article's own text never mentions the word persimmon, but the PDF does.

40
00:03:03,416 --> 00:03:08,749
The index records the names of its fields when it is saved, and Reset saves it.

41
00:03:08,749 --> 00:03:14,021
In Search, Indexes, open Actions on the Articles index and choose Reset, which

42
00:03:14,021 --> 00:03:17,096
restarts the indexing of all content items.

43
00:03:17,576 --> 00:03:20,359
The list reloads with a success message.

44
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:23,746
Then select Edit on the Articles index to open it.

45
00:03:24,213 --> 00:03:29,342
Under Default Query Settings, Query field names lists the fields of the index.

46
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:31,784
Two belong to the Documents field:

47
00:03:31,784 --> 00:03:35,753
one name ends in MediaText, and the other ends in FileText.

48
00:03:36,212 --> 00:03:42,661
FileText holds the text read from the files, and MediaText holds the descriptions typed by editors.

49
00:03:42,661 --> 00:03:47,943
The search covers only the selected names, or every field when none is selected.

50
00:03:47,943 --> 00:03:52,242
Let's select both, leave the other fields as they are, and save.

51
00:03:56,439 --> 00:04:02,121
The search page of the site uses the Default search index from Settings, Search, Site Search.

52
00:04:02,121 --> 00:04:04,359
Here, that is the Articles index.

53
00:04:04,823 --> 00:04:09,143
Let's open the search page of the site, type persimmon, and select Search.

54
00:04:10,171 --> 00:04:15,260
The Autumn Garden Guide article is listed, although the word appears only in

55
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:17,061
the PDF attached to it.

56
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:23,054
Only some formats are read.

57
00:04:23,054 --> 00:04:27,346
Word documents in the doc format and Excel workbooks can be attached, but

58
00:04:27,346 --> 00:04:30,281
no feature reads them, so they add no file text.

59
00:04:31,031 --> 00:04:35,375
Text and Markdown files can be attached only when the site allows those

60
00:04:35,375 --> 00:04:40,341
file types in its Media configuration, and the default list does not include them.

61
00:04:42,341 --> 00:04:46,267
Enable the feature for a file type, include the Media Field in the

62
00:04:46,267 --> 00:04:50,903
index, update the index, and the words inside attached files become searchable.

63
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:52,321
Thanks for watching.

